---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Key Rotation">
  <h1>Key Rotation</h1>
  <p>
    Rotating the JWT signing key without breaking in-flight access tokens
    is a four-step process. The whole point of the
    <code>kid</code> claim and JWKS is that you can introduce a new key
    while the old one still verifies.
  </p>

  <h2>Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Production deployment uses <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS</code> (not the auto-generated dev key).</li>
    <li>Downstream services cache JWKS with a known refresh interval (e.g. 12 hours).</li>
    <li>You know <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> for the deployment.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Step 1 — Generate a new key</h2>
  <Code code={`openssl genrsa -out k-2026-08.pem 2048`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Step 2 — Add the new key as inactive</h2>
  <p>
    Update <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS</code> so the ring contains both the
    current active key and the new one (inactive). After redeploy, the
    new key appears in JWKS but is not yet used to sign anything.
  </p>
  <Code code={`[
  {
    "kid": "k-2026-05",
    "private_key_pem": "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n...current...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n",
    "active": true
  },
  {
    "kid": "k-2026-08",
    "private_key_pem": "-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n...new...\\n-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----\\n",
    "active": false
  }
]`} lang="json" title="GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS — pre-flip" />
  <p>
    Verify the new key is published:
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -s https://auth.acme.com/.well-known/jwks.json | jq '.keys[].kid'
# "k-2026-05"
# "k-2026-08"`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Step 3 — Wait for downstream caches</h2>
  <p>
    Downstream services need to pick up the new key from JWKS before any
    token signed by it reaches them. Wait at least one cache TTL — for
    the recommended 12-hour interval, wait 24 hours to be safe.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 4 — Flip the active flag</h2>
  <p>
    Promote the new key. After redeploy, all newly-issued access tokens
    are signed with <code>k-2026-08</code>. Tokens already in flight,
    signed by <code>k-2026-05</code>, continue to verify because that key
    is still in JWKS.
  </p>
  <Code code={`[
  {
    "kid": "k-2026-05",
    "private_key_pem": "...",
    "active": false
  },
  {
    "kid": "k-2026-08",
    "private_key_pem": "...",
    "active": true
  }
]`} lang="json" title="GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS — post-flip" />

  <h2>Step 5 — Retire the old key</h2>
  <p>
    Wait <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> (default 15 min) plus a
    safety margin. After that point, no extant access token was signed by
    <code>k-2026-05</code>, so it can be removed entirely.
  </p>
  <Code code={`[
  {
    "kid": "k-2026-08",
    "private_key_pem": "...",
    "active": true
  }
]`} lang="json" title="GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS — final" />

  <h2>Emergency rotation</h2>
  <p>
    If a key is suspected compromised:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Drop it from the ring entirely (don't bother with the inactive
      step — every token signed by it should be rejected immediately).
    </li>
    <li>
      Bring <code>GATEWAY_JWT_EXPIRY_SECONDS</code> very low (e.g. 60)
      temporarily so existing access tokens expire fast.
    </li>
    <li>
      Run <code>SignOutEverywhere</code> on every user (no admin RPC for
      this; do it directly via EntDB to delete every refresh token).
    </li>
    <li>
      Restore the normal JWT expiry once the kill is confirmed.
    </li>
  </ol>
</DocsLayout>
